ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE LIQUOR TAX ACT

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Liquor Tax Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Scope of Liquor)
(1) "Those prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 1 (b) of the Liquor Tax Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following: <Amended 2022.2.15>
1. Pharmaceuticals under the Pharmaceutical Affairs Act with an alcohol content of less than six degrees;
2. Food manufactured for use in enhancing flavor by being added during the cooking of other food, among liquor falling under subparagraph 4 (c) of the attached Table to the Act, with a nonvolatile content of at least 30 degrees.
(2) "Those prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 1 (c) of the Act means products whose liquor-manufacturing raw materials are removed from manufacturing premises or declared for import while contained in a container and then ferment within that container without the addition of further raw materials, ultimately becoming a beverage under Article 2, subparagraph 1 (b) of the Act.
(3) The type of liquor under paragraph (2) shall be determined on the basis of the final product.

Article 3 (Specifications of Liquor, etc.)
(1) The liquor or materials that may be mixed with or added to each type of liquor pursuant to Articles 5 and 6 of, and the attached Table to, the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 1.
(2) The alcohol content, in degrees, for each type of liquor under Articles 5 and 6 of, and the attached Table to, the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 2.
(3) The quantities of raw materials used, filtration methods, etc. for each type of liquor when manufacturing liquor pursuant to Articles 5 and 6 of, and the attached Table to, the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 3.

CHAPTER II TAX BASE AND TAX RATES

Article 4 (Calculation of Quantities of Liquor)
The quantities of liquor under Article 7 (1) of the Act shall be calculated according to the following classifications:
1. Taxable quantity of beer manufactured by a small-scale liquor manufacturer under Article 2 (3) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc. (hereinafter referred to as a "small-scale liquor manufacturer") [excluding liquor manufactured under contract pursuant to Article 3 (8) of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "contract-manufactured liquor"); hereinafter the same shall apply in this subparagraph]: The quantity obtained by multiplying the quantity actually removed from the liquor manufacturing premises during the relevant brewing year by the recognized ratio for the following classifications: Provided, That, where the weight of rice used among the raw-material grains for beer is at least 20 percent of the combined weight of materials containing starch, sugars or caramel, and germinated cereal grains, the recognized ratio for each quantity removed shall be 30 percent:
(a) The first quantity removed, up to 200 kiloliters: 40 percent;
(b) The quantity removed after the quantity under item (a), exceeding 200 kiloliters and up to 500 kiloliters: 60 percent;
(c) The quantity removed after the quantity under item (b), exceeding 500 kiloliters: 80 percent.
2. Taxable quantity of beer manufactured by small and medium enterprises falling under item (a) (excluding small-scale liquor manufacturers), among small and medium enterprises under Article 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Restriction of Special Taxation Act that meet the facility standards under subparagraph 1 (d) of attached Table 1 to the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc. and have obtained a liquor manufacturing license pursuant to Article 3 of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc. (excluding contract-manufactured liquor): The quantity under item (b):
(a) Eligible small and medium enterprises: The following small and medium enterprises:
1) A small and medium enterprise that has newly obtained a beer manufacturing license during the relevant brewing year;
2) A small and medium enterprise whose quantity removed during the immediately preceding brewing year was not more than 3,000 kiloliters;
(b) Taxable quantity: The quantity according to the following classifications:
1) The first quantity removed, up to 500 kiloliters: The quantity obtained by multiplying the quantity actually removed from the liquor manufacturing premises by 70 percent;
2) The quantity removed after the quantity under subitem 1), exceeding 500 kiloliters: The quantity actually removed from the liquor manufacturing premises.
3. Taxable quantity of takju manufactured by a small-scale liquor manufacturer (excluding contract-manufactured liquor): The quantity obtained by multiplying the quantity actually removed from the liquor manufacturing premises during the relevant brewing year by the recognized ratio for the following classifications:
(a) The first quantity removed, up to five kiloliters: 60 percent;
(b) The quantity removed after the quantity under item (a), exceeding five kiloliters: 80 percent.
4. Taxable quantities other than those prescribed in subparagraphs 1 through 3: The quantity removed from liquor manufacturing premises or declared for import.

Article 5 (Calculation of Liquor Prices)
(1) The price of liquor under Article 7 (2) 1 of the Act shall be the amount calculated according to the following classifications (hereinafter referred to in this Article as the "manufacturing premises selling price"), less the amount calculated by multiplying the standard selling ratio under Article 5-2 by the manufacturing premises selling price: <Amended 2023.12.14, 2025.2.28>
1. Price of liquor removed from liquor manufacturing premises through ordinary transaction methods (meaning methods other than those specified in subparagraph 2): The price at which the liquor manufacturer sells ordinary wholesale quantities through ordinary transaction methods (hereinafter referred to as the "ordinary price");
2. Price of liquor removed from liquor manufacturing premises through special transaction methods according to the following classifications: The amounts specified in the following items:
(a) Where liquor is removed on credit at a price higher than the ordinary price: An amount equivalent to the price at which it is removed;
(b) Where liquor is removed through advance sales at a price lower than the ordinary price, or is removed after deducting a fixed amount from the ordinary price in accordance with commercial practice: An amount equivalent to the ordinary price;
(c) Where a fixed amount is refunded to the purchaser after a certain period has elapsed following removal of liquor: An amount equivalent to the price at the time of the initial removal;
(d) Where liquor is removed free of charge: The ordinary price of liquor of the same specifications and capacity as the relevant liquor; where the price cannot be calculated on the basis of liquor of the same specifications and capacity, the amount obtained by adding an amount equivalent to an ordinary profit (meaning 10 percent of manufacturing cost; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) to the manufacturing cost of that liquor. In such cases, notwithstanding accounting concepts, the manufacturing cost shall be the total amount consisting of the portions of raw-material costs, auxiliary-material costs, labor costs, expenses, and general administrative expenses (including selling expenses) that should be allocated to the relevant liquor;
(e) Where a liquor manufacturer removes liquor free of charge or at a price lower than the ordinary price to a sales outlet having any of the following special relationships: An amount equivalent to the ordinary price:
1) A sales outlet specially established by a liquor manufacturer to sell directly the liquor it produces (including a storage depot under Article 18 (1) 2 of the Act);
2) A sales outlet operated by a person having a relationship with the liquor manufacturer falling under Article 98 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act or any subparagraph of Article 2 (8) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act;
(f) Where transportation charges are included in the price of liquor (including cases where transportation charges are received separately from the liquor price), but the transportation charge received by the liquor manufacturer from the other party is the same irrespective of distance: An amount equivalent to the price including those transportation charges;
(g) Where a small-scale liquor manufacturer manufactures yakju or cheongju (excluding the manufacture of contract-manufactured liquor; hereinafter the same shall apply in this item): The amounts according to the following classifications:
1) Where liquor is sold pursuant to subparagraph 1 (a) through (c) of the Notes to subparagraph 4 of attached Table 1 to the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc.: The amount obtained by multiplying the sum of the manufacturing cost calculated on the basis of the ordinary manufacturing quantity (meaning the manufacturing cost under the latter part of item (d)) and an amount equivalent to an ordinary profit by the price ratio specified below according to the taxable quantity of yakju or cheongju removed during the relevant brewing year:
(i) The first quantity removed, up to five kiloliters: 60 percent;
(ii) The quantity removed after the quantity under subitem (i), exceeding five kiloliters: 80 percent;
2) Where liquor is sold pursuant to subparagraph 1 (d) of the Notes to subparagraph 4 of attached Table 1 to the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc.: The amount obtained by multiplying the ordinary price (where liquor is removed through a transaction method under items (a) through (f), meaning the amount prescribed in the relevant item) by the price ratio specified below according to the taxable quantity of yakju or cheongju removed during the relevant brewing year:
(i) The first quantity removed, up to five kiloliters: 60 percent;
(ii) The quantity removed after the quantity under subitem (i), exceeding five kiloliters: 80 percent;
(h) Where traditional liquor is removed at a price higher than the ordinary price through mail-order sales under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the Act on the Consumer Protection in Electronic Commerce, etc.: An amount equivalent to the ordinary price.
(2) The price of imported liquor under Article 7 (2) 2 of the Act shall be the price at the time of the import declaration pursuant to Article 241 of the Customs Act (meaning the sum of the customs value and customs duty).
(3) When calculating the price of liquor pursuant to paragraph (1), where liquor is removed under a direct contract with a unit of the Armed Forces or a sales institution recognized as having public credibility, the contract amount shall be the ordinary price.

Article 5-2 (Standard Selling Ratios)
(1) The standard selling ratio under the part of Article 5 (1) preceding its subparagraphs shall be a ratio publicly notified by the Commissioner of the National Tax Service following the procedures prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, taking into account the average selling costs (meaning costs incurred after the manufacturing stage), etc. surveyed according to the type of liquor and the liquor manufacturer's selling method. In such cases, the Commissioner of the National Tax Service may publicly notify standard selling ratios separately by type of liquor (meaning liquor classified pursuant to Article 5 (1) of the Act). <Amended 2025.12.30>
(2) The period for applying a standard selling ratio under paragraph (1) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Commissioner of the National Tax Service within a range of two years.

Article 6 (Calculation of Container Prices and Packaging Costs, etc.)
(1) When calculating the price of liquor pursuant to Article 5 (1), where liquor that is not removed in bottles is removed at an amount after deducting the container price and packaging costs on condition that the container or packaging be returned to the manufacturing premises of that liquor, that amount shall be the ordinary price.
(2) Where a liquor manufacturer receives a resource circulation deposit pursuant to Article 15-2 of the Act on the Promotion of Saving and Recycling of Resources in order to recover or reuse reusable containers or packaging when removing liquor, that resource circulation deposit shall not be included in the price at the time of removal under Article 7 (2) 1 of the Act. <Amended 2021.11.23>
(3) "Container prices or packaging costs prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 7 (3) of the Act means the following:
1. The price of ceramic bottles specially manufactured for containing liquor and packaging for those bottles;
2. The price of electronic identification tags manufactured to identify product information wirelessly and removed while attached to liquor containers or packaging;
3. All container prices and packaging costs for traditional liquor.
(4) Container prices and packaging costs under paragraph (3) shall be calculated separately from the price of liquor.

Article 7 (Tax Rates)
(1) Deleted <2024.2.29>
(2) "Liquor prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 8 (3) of the Act means traditional liquor specified in Article 5 (1) 2 and 3 of the Act and subparagraph 4 (a) through (e) of the attached Table to the Act (hereinafter referred to in this Article and attached Table 2 as "liquor eligible for reduced tax rates") that meets the criteria according to the following classifications (hereinafter referred to in this Article as the "criteria for application of reduced tax rates"): <Amended 2025.2.28>
1. Liquor under Article 5 (1) 2 of the Act or subparagraph 4 (b) of the attached Table to the Act: Not more than 1,000 kiloliters shall be manufactured, based on the taxable quantity removed during the immediately preceding brewing year;
2. Liquor under Article 5 (1) 3 of the Act or subparagraph 4 (a) or (c) through (e) of the attached Table to the Act: Not more than 500 kiloliters shall be manufactured, based on the taxable quantity removed during the immediately preceding brewing year.
(3) When applying paragraph (2), liquor according to the following classifications shall be deemed to meet the criteria for application of reduced tax rates:
1. Where a manufacturing license for liquor eligible for reduced tax rates is newly obtained: Liquor manufactured by the relevant liquor manufacturer during the brewing year in which the manufacturing license is obtained;
2. Where the taxable quantity of liquor eligible for reduced tax rates removed during the immediately preceding brewing year exceeds the criteria for application of reduced tax rates for the first time: Liquor manufactured during the brewing year in which the grounds for exceeding the criteria arise and through the following two brewing years.
(4) "Quantity removed prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 8 (3) of the Act means the quantities according to the following classifications: <Amended 2025.2.28>
1. Liquor under Article 5 (1) 2 of the Act or subparagraph 4 (b) of the attached Table to the Act: The first 400 kiloliters removed out of the taxable quantity removed during the relevant brewing year;
2. Liquor under Article 5 (1) 3 of the Act or subparagraph 4 (a) or (c) through (e) of the attached Table to the Act: The first 200 kiloliters removed out of the taxable quantity removed during the relevant brewing year.
(5) When calculating tax using a reduced tax rate pursuant to Article 8 (3) of the Act, the calculation shall be made by subtracting the tax rate reduction calculated according to the following classifications from the tax rate under paragraph (1) or (2) of that Article (hereinafter referred to in this paragraph as the "basic tax rate"). In such cases, amounts of less than 100 won in the tax rate for liquor under Article 5 (1) 2 (a) of the Act shall be disregarded. <Added 2025.2.28>
1. Liquor under Article 5 (1) 2 of the Act or subparagraph 4 (b) of the attached Table to the Act: Calculated by multiplying the basic tax rate by the ratio according to the following classifications:
(a) The first quantity removed, up to 200 kiloliters, out of the taxable quantity removed during the relevant brewing year: 50 percent;
(b) The quantity removed after the quantity under item (a), exceeding 200 kiloliters and up to 400 kiloliters: 30 percent.
2. Liquor under Article 5 (1) 3 of the Act or subparagraph 4 (a) or (c) through (e) of the attached Table to the Act: Calculated by multiplying the basic tax rate by the ratio according to the following classifications:
(a) The first quantity removed, up to 100 kiloliters, out of the taxable quantity removed during the relevant brewing year: 50 percent;
(b) The quantity removed after the quantity under item (a), exceeding 100 kiloliters and up to 200 kiloliters: 30 percent.

CHAPTER III FILING AND PAYMENT

Article 8 (Filing of Tax Base Returns)
Returns under Article 9 (1) and (2) of the Act shall be filed using the liquor tax base return prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy. <Amended 2025.12.30>

Article 9 (Payment)
A person who pays liquor tax pursuant to Article 10 (1) of the Act shall pay the amount of tax payable to the competent tax office head, the Bank of Korea (including its agents), or a postal office by the deadline prescribed in Article 11 of the Act.

Article 10 (Method of Calculating Quantities of Liquor Removed)
(1) In calculating the quantity of liquor removed from liquor manufacturing premises pursuant to Article 10 of the Act, actual loss occurring during sediment separation, filtration, storage, filling into containers, and removal may be recognized as a reduction in the quantity of liquor verified pursuant to Article 27 of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc.: Provided, That, in the case of liquor to which a tax payment certification label under Article 22 of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc. has not been affixed, actual loss may be recognized within the following limits:
1. Spirit: Not more than 1/100 of the verified quantity;
2. Cheongju: Not more than 5/100 of the verified quantity;
3. Beer: Not more than 35/1,000 of the verified quantity (not more than 70/1,000 in the case of beer manufactured by a small-scale liquor manufacturer);
4. Liquor other than that prescribed in subparagraphs 1 through 3: Not more than 2/100 of the verified quantity.
(2) Where undiluted liquor (meaning liquor not mixed with water) is stored in wooden casks, actual loss of not more than 4/100 per year may additionally be recognized, in addition to the actual loss under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1). <Amended 2025.2.28>

CHAPTER IV DETERMINATION, CORRECTION, COLLECTION, AND REFUNDS

Article 11 (Methods of Determination by Estimation)
An estimation under the proviso to Article 12 (3) of the Act shall be made by the method specified in the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the Commissioner of the National Tax Service has surveyed ratios in respect of the following relationships, taking into account the type of business, region, etc.: A method of calculation applying such ratios;
(a) The relationship between the quantities and values of all or part of the raw materials and subsidiary materials used and the production quantities and sales amounts;
(b) The relationship between the quantities and values of all or part of the human and physical facilities related to the business (meaning employees, business premises, vehicles, water supply, electricity, etc.) and the production quantities and sales amounts;
(c) The relationship between the average inventory quantities and inventory values for a specified period and the production quantities and sales amounts;
(d) The relationship between gross profits on sales or value added for a specified period and sales amounts;
2. Where the ratios under subparagraph 1 can be calculated directly for a business operator subject to determination or correction by estimation: A method of calculation applying the directly calculated ratios;
3. In cases other than those under subparagraphs 1 and 2: A method of calculation by comparison with other persons engaged in the same business whose bookkeeping is deemed proper, who have faithfully filed returns, and who have not been subject to determination and correction pursuant to Article 12 (1) and (2) of the Act.

Article 12 (Calculation of Prices, etc. of Liquor Deemed Removed)
(1) The prices (meaning prices forming the tax base; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) of liquor deemed removed pursuant to subparagraphs 1, 2, and 4 of Article 15 and Article 16 of the Act (hereinafter in this Article referred to as "liquor deemed removed") shall be the ordinary prices for liquor of the same specifications and capacity as the relevant liquor in the month in which the date of deemed removal falls or the immediately preceding month.
(2) In applying paragraph (1), where liquor deemed removed is kept without being placed in containers or packaged, the quantity of such liquor (meaning the quantity forming the tax base) shall be converted according to the type of container with the largest sales volume during the month in which the date of deemed removal falls or the immediately preceding month.
(3) The prices of liquor deemed removed pursuant to subparagraph 3 of Article 15 of the Act shall be the amounts realized through a public sale, auction, or bankruptcy proceedings.

Article 13 (Exclusions from Deemed Removal)
(1) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to subparagraph 2 of Article 15 of the Act means the following cases:
1. Where the competent tax office head cancels a liquor manufacturing license and, pursuant to Article 12 (3) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc., fixes a period and allows the manufacture, removal, and other necessary acts in relation to semi-finished products existing at the liquor manufacturing premises to continue;
2. Where, when submitting an application for cancellation of a license pursuant to Article 14 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc., a liquor manufacturer obtains approval from the competent tax office head to continue removing liquor existing at the liquor manufacturing premises for a specified removal period.
(2) Where manufacture, removal, and other necessary acts are not completed within the period under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), the main clause of subparagraph 2 of Article 15 of the Act shall apply, with the date on which such period expires deemed the date of cancellation of the license.

Article 14 (Application for Approval for Removal without Payment of Tax)
(1) A person who intends to remove liquor falling under any subparagraph of Article 17 (1) of the Act from liquor manufacturing premises or a bonded area shall, when removing the liquor, submit an application stating the following matters to the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head and obtain approval:
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Place of removal;
3. Quantities and prices of liquor, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
4. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax;
5. Place of receipt;
6. Personal particulars of the recipient;
7. Scheduled date of removal;
8. Deadline for submission of the certificate of receipt;
9. Grounds for the application.
(2) The documents specified in the following subparagraphs shall be attached to an application under paragraph (1):
1. In cases falling under Article 17 (1) 1 of the Act: A domestic letter of credit or a certificate of purchase under the Foreign Trade Act;
2. In cases falling under Article 17 (1) 2 of the Act: Documents capable of certifying that the liquor is for use as a raw material.
(3) Where the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head who has received an application under paragraph (1) grants approval for removal, such head shall issue a written approval to the applicant within 5 days from the date of receipt of the application and notify the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head having jurisdiction over the place of receipt of that fact. <Amended 2026.2.27>
(4) When issuing a written approval pursuant to paragraph (3), the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head shall require the applicant to submit a certificate of receipt (meaning a certificate issued pursuant to Article 16 (2) to a person who has filed a report of receipt; hereinafter the same shall apply) by the date falling 2 months after the date of removal of the liquor.

Article 15 (Application for Approval of Loss Due to Disasters, etc.)
(1) A person who seeks the application of Article 17 (4) of the Act shall submit an application stating the following matters to the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head by the deadline for submission of the certificate of receipt under Article 14 (4) (hereinafter referred to as the "deadline for submission of the certificate of receipt") and obtain approval: <Amended 2022.2.15>
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Location of the liquor manufacturing premises, approval number, and date of approval (in the case of imported liquor, the name of the exporting country);
3. Quantities and prices of the lost liquor, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
4. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax;
5. Date, place, and cause of the loss, and the method of handling the lost liquor;
6. Deadline for submission of the certificate of receipt.
(2) A person who seeks the application of the proviso to Article 20 (3) of the Act shall submit an application stating the following matters to the competent tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises or the competent customs office head within 30 days from the date on which the relevant cause occurs and obtain approval:
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Location of the liquor manufacturing premises (in the case of imported liquor, the name of the exporting country);
3. Place where the liquor was lost;
4. Date of approval of exemption from liquor tax and approval number;
5. Quantities and prices of liquor, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
6. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax.
(3) In cases under paragraph (1) or (2), where the place of loss is outside the jurisdiction of the competent tax office or the competent customs office having jurisdiction over the location of the liquor manufacturing premises, a certificate stating the following matters, issued by the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head having jurisdiction over the place of loss, shall be attached to the application under paragraph (1) or (2):
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Location of the liquor manufacturing premises (in the case of imported liquor, the name of the exporting country);
3. Name of the tax office (customs office) that approved the tax exemption or removal, date of approval, and approval number;
4. Quantities and prices of the lost liquor, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
5. Date, place, and cause of the loss;
6. Personal particulars of the person who removed the liquor.

Article 16 (Reporting and Certification of Receipt)
(1) A person who intends to report the fact of receipt pursuant to Article 17 (5) of the Act shall submit a report stating the following matters to the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head having jurisdiction over the place of receipt: <Amended 2022.2.15>
1. Personal particulars of the person filing the report;
2. Approval number and date of approval;
3. Quantities and prices of liquor, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
4. Place of receipt;
5. Reason for receipt;
6. Date of receipt;
7. Personal particulars of the person who removed the liquor;
8. Deadline for submission of the certificate of receipt;
9. Other matters for reference.
(2) The competent tax office head or the competent customs office head having jurisdiction over the place of receipt who has received a report under paragraph (1) shall issue a certificate of receipt to the person who filed the report of receipt within 5 days from the date of receipt of the report. <Amended 2026.2.27>
(3) A person who has removed liquor under Article 17 (1) of the Act from liquor manufacturing premises or a bonded area shall submit a certificate of receipt to the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head by the deadline for submission of the certificate of receipt.

Article 17 (Tax Credits and Refunds for Returned Liquor)
(1) "Discontinuance of production prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18 (1) 1 of the Act means a case where a liquor manufacturer has not manufactured a liquor product reported pursuant to Article 9 (1) of the Act for at least 2 consecutive brewing years.
(2) "Discontinuance of importation prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 18 (1) 2 of the Act means a case where a person who imports liquor has not imported a liquor product reported pursuant to Article 9 (3) of the Act for at least 2 consecutive brewing years.
(3) A person who intends to apply for a tax credit or refund pursuant to Article 18 (2) of the Act shall submit an application stating the following matters to the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head, together with a return under Article 8 or Article 9 (3) of the Act:
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Quantities and prices, by type, capacity, and alcohol content, of liquor returned, disposed of (meaning disposal at the location of the head office or storage depot of the person who filed the import declaration; hereinafter the same shall apply), damaged, or lost;
3. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax to be credited or refunded;
4. Reasons for the return or disposal;
5. Dates of removal or importation and dates of return or disposal.
(4) The following documents shall be attached to an application under paragraph (3):
1. Documents certifying that the liquor was returned or disposed of due to deterioration, poor quality, discontinuance of production or importation, or other unavoidable reasons, or that it was damaged in the course of distribution or lost due to a natural disaster;
2. Documents certifying the amount of tax already paid or payable.
(5) The documents under paragraph (4) 1 shall be documents in which the competent tax office head, the competent customs office head, or an administrative agency vested with the relevant authority has confirmed that the liquor was returned or disposed of due to deterioration, poor quality, discontinuance of production or importation, or other unavoidable reasons, or that it was damaged in the course of distribution or lost due to a natural disaster.

Article 18 (Tax Credits and Refunds for Liquor Used as a Raw Material)
(1) A person who intends to receive a credit for the amount of liquor tax pursuant to Article 19 (1) of the Act shall submit an application stating the following matters to the competent tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises, together with a return under Article 8:
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Quantities and prices of the liquor removed, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
3. Quantities, prices, suppliers, and dates of purchase, by type, capacity, and alcohol content, of the liquor used as a raw material in the liquor removed;
4. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax to be credited.
(2) A person who intends to receive a credit or refund of the amount of liquor tax pursuant to Article 19 (3) of the Act (excluding a person falling under paragraph (1)) shall submit an application stating the following matters, with documents certifying facts falling under Article 20 (1) 1 through 5 of the Act attached thereto, to the competent tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises, together with a return under Article 8:
1. Personal particulars of the applicant;
2. Quantities and prices of liquor exported or supplied, by type, capacity, and alcohol content;
3. Quantities, prices, suppliers, and dates of purchase, by type, capacity, and alcohol content, of the liquor used as a raw material in the liquor exported or supplied;
4. Dates of exportation or supply;
5. Export destinations or recipients of supply;
6. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax to be credited or refunded.
(3) The competent tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises who has received an application for a refund of the amount of liquor tax under paragraph (2) shall make the refund within 10 days after the payment deadline under Article 11 of the Act has passed.

CHAPTER V TAX EXEMPTION

Article 19 (Definition of Export)
(1) "Export" in Article 20 (1) 1 of the Act means any of the following:

1. Removal of liquor to a foreign country (including liquor supplied under a local letter of credit or a purchase confirmation under the Foreign Trade Act);

2. Supply of liquor to foreign-going vessels or aircraft, or to deep-sea fishing vessels;

3. Sale of liquor to persons departing from the country within bonded areas at entry and departure ports.

(2) Liquor delivered to any of the following institutions or directly imported by such institutions for official use pursuant to Article 20 (1) 4 or (2) 1 of the Act shall be exempt from liquor tax:

1. Any of the following institutions (hereinafter referred to in this Article as "foreign missions in Korea"):

(a) Embassies;

(b) Legations;

(c) Consulates-general;

(d) Consulates (excluding honorary consulates);

2. Foreign institutions in Korea accorded treatment equivalent to that of the institutions referred to in subparagraph 1 pursuant to agreements;

3. A foreign correspondents' club established with permission from the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism.

(3) Exemption from liquor tax under Article 20 (1) 5 of the Act shall be limited to liquor served for drinking within a lounge for foreign seafarers.

(4) Liquor directly imported for consumption by foreign nationals working at foreign missions in Korea who hold the status of public officials of their respective countries, and by foreign nationals working at foreign missions in Korea or other equivalent institutions, pursuant to Article 20 (2) 2 of the Act shall be exempt from liquor tax.

Article 20 (Application for Approval of Tax Exemption for Liquor for Export or Delivery)
(1) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (1) 1 through 3 and 5 of the Act shall, before removal of the liquor, submit an application stating the following matters to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises and obtain approval. In such cases, where the liquor manufacturer and the person exporting or delivering the liquor are different persons and the liquor is exported or delivered directly from the manufacturing premises, the liquor manufacturer and the person exporting or delivering the liquor shall apply jointly:

1. Particulars of the applicant;

2. Location of the manufacturing premises of the liquor to be exported or delivered;

3. Quantities and prices of the liquor to be exported or delivered, classified by type, capacity, and alcohol content;

4. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax;

5. Year, month, and day of export or delivery;

6. Port of export and place of import, or recipient and place of delivery;

7. Method of shipment of the liquor for export.

(2) A person who has obtained approval of exemption from liquor tax pursuant to paragraph (1) shall submit an export declaration certificate, a certificate of delivery, a certificate of completion of loading onto a vessel or aircraft, or documents prescribed by the Commissioner of the National Tax Service in lieu thereof, to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises within the period determined by that tax office head, not exceeding three months from the date of approval. In such cases, where the tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises deems that unavoidable reasons exist, the tax office head may extend that period by up to three months.

(3) Submission of the export declaration certificate referred to in paragraph (2) shall be replaced by verification by the competent tax office head through the joint use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act: Provided, That, if the person who has obtained approval of exemption under paragraph (1) does not consent to such verification, the person shall be required to submit the relevant document.

(4) A person intending to deliver liquor exempt from liquor tax under Article 20 (1) 4 of the Act shall remove the liquor in accordance with a recommendation for purchase of tax-exempt liquor issued by the competent Minister upon application by the relevant institution referred to in that subparagraph.

(5) A person who has delivered liquor pursuant to paragraph (4) shall attach documents proving delivery to the relevant institution to the return under Article 8.

(6) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (1) 6 of the Act shall attach to the return under Article 8 documents issued by the inspection institution proving that the liquor was collected.

(7) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (1) 7 of the Act shall attach to the return under Article 8 documents proving that the liquor is to be used for the public presentation of intangible heritage, issued by the Administrator of Korea Heritage Service or, in the case of intangible heritage of a Si/Do only, by a Special City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor. <Amended on May 7, 2024>

(8) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (1) 8 of the Act shall submit to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises an application stating the following matters, accompanied by documents issued by the Minister of Food and Drug Safety proving the relevant facts, and obtain approval:

1. Particulars of the applicant;

2. Name of the exporting country, in the case of imported pharmaceutical raw materials;

3. Type, alcohol content, quantity, and price of the pharmaceutical raw materials;

4. Intended use of the pharmaceutical raw materials;

5. Year, month, and day of removal;

6. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax.

(9) Where a person who has not obtained approval of tax exemption under paragraph (1) seeks exemption from liquor tax under Article 20 (1) 1 of the Act, the person shall submit to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises the tax base return for the relevant quarter, accompanied by the documents under paragraph (2) and the documents prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, by the 25th day of the month following the last month of the quarter in which the date of removal of the liquor falls. <Amended on December 30, 2025>

Article 21 (Application for Approval of Purchase of Tax-Exempt Liquor for Export)
(1) A person intending to purchase liquor for the purposes specified in Article 19 (1) 2 and 3 shall submit an application stating the following matters to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the sales premises or the competent customs office head and obtain approval:

1. Particulars of the applicant;

2. Location of the sales premises;

3. Type, specifications, capacity, quantity, and price of the liquor to be purchased;

4. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax;

5. Place and date of purchase;

6. Year, month, and day on which a liquor sales business license or a certificate of verification of a report was obtained.

(2) Where a person who has obtained approval under paragraph (1) purchases liquor and receives it at the sales premises, the person shall submit a report on receipt of tax-exempt liquor for export, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the sales premises or the competent customs office head within five days from the date of receipt. <Amended on December 30, 2025>

(3) Upon receiving a report under paragraph (2), the tax office head having jurisdiction over the sales premises or the competent customs office head shall verify receipt of the liquor and notify the tax office head having jurisdiction over the place of removal of that fact without delay.

Article 22 (Tax Exemption for Spirit)
(1) Exemption from liquor tax on spirit under Article 20 (1) 9 of the Act shall apply to spirit used in quantities of at least 20 liters on each occasion to manufacture the goods specified in attached Table 4 or for industrial purposes other than food or beverage use. <Amended on February 15, 2022>

(2) To obtain exemption from liquor tax on spirit falling under paragraph (1), approval from the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head shall be obtained: Provided, That approval need not be obtained for synthetic spirit for industrial use (meaning spirit manufactured by synthesis using ethylene as a raw material). <Newly Inserted on February 15, 2022>

(3) Spirit removed with approval under the main clause of paragraph (2) shall be denatured by a method designated by the competent tax office head, the competent customs office head, or the tax office head having jurisdiction over the place of receipt so that it cannot be consumed as a beverage, and shall undergo testing under Article 27 of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc.: Provided, That spirit used to manufacture goods for export, reagents, goods for testing and research, government supplies, or medical or pharmaceutical goods need not be denatured. <Amended on February 15, 2022>

(4) The procedure for approval of exemption from liquor tax under the main clause of paragraph (2) shall follow the order specified in the following subparagraphs: <Amended on February 15, 2022; December 30, 2025>

1. A person intending to purchase spirit falling under paragraph (1) shall obtain certification as an actual end user by submitting to the tax office head having jurisdiction over the person's location an application stating the name of the relevant liquor manufacturing premises or the name of the manufacturer, the year, month, and day of purchase, the alcohol content, quantity, and intended use (where the person intending to purchase the spirit holds a spirit retail business license under Article 8 (2) 7 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Liquor Licenses, etc. and intends to manufacture reagent alcohol, the application shall be accompanied by a manufacturing process diagram and a description of the manufacturing method for reagent alcohol). In such cases, certification as an actual end user may be obtained for a three-month quantity at one time for reagent alcohol packaged in containers of not more than 10 liters and used for research purposes by schools under the Elementary and Secondary Education Act and the Higher Education Act, institutions and organizations specified in the subparagraphs of Article 14 (1) of the Basic Research Promotion and Technology Development Support Act, and research institutions that are non-profit corporations;

2. A person intending to remove spirit for a person who has obtained certification as an actual end user under subparagraph 1 (hereinafter referred to as an "actual end user") shall obtain approval for removal of the spirit by submitting to the competent tax office head or the competent customs office head an application prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy and the documents certifying the actual end user under subparagraph 1;

3. The tax office head having jurisdiction over the place of removal or the competent customs office head who receives an application for approval under subparagraph 2 shall not approve removal of the spirit in the absence of certification as an actual end user under subparagraph 1, and, when approving spirit to be used to manufacture goods falling under subparagraph 47 of attached Table 4, shall impose the condition that the spirit be used within six months from the date of approval;

4. Where the tax office head having jurisdiction over the place of removal or the competent customs office head grants approval under subparagraph 2, the tax office head or customs office head shall notify the tax office head having jurisdiction over the actual end user's location of the details of the approval and whether the spirit has been denatured, without delay;

5. Upon receiving notification under subparagraph 3, the tax office head having jurisdiction over the actual end user's location shall verify the denaturing of the spirit and its use or disposition by the actual end user: Provided, That, in the case of goods falling under subparagraph 47 of attached Table 4, the tax office head shall verify the export declaration certificate through the joint use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act, and, if the actual end user does not consent to such verification, shall require the actual end user to submit the export declaration certificate or documents in lieu thereof within one month after export.

(5) Where spirit under Article 20 (1) 9 of the Act that has been exempted from liquor tax is not used for the relevant purpose after its receipt or removal from a bonded area (including where it is not used within the period under paragraph (3) 3), the place of receipt or taking delivery shall be deemed the manufacturing premises, and the business operator at the place of receipt or taking delivery shall be deemed the liquor manufacturer, and the liquor tax shall be collected without delay. <Amended on February 15, 2022>

Article 23 (Application for Approval of Tax Exemption for Imported Liquor)
(1) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (2) 1 and 2 of the Act shall submit to the competent customs office head an application stating the following matters, accompanied by a recommendation for purchase of tax-exempt liquor issued by the Minister of Foreign Affairs, and obtain approval:

1. Particulars of the applicant;

2. Name of the exporting country;

3. Type, alcohol content, quantity, and price of the imported liquor;

4. Intended use of the imported liquor;

5. Year, month, and day of removal;

6. Amount equivalent to the liquor tax.

(2) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (2) 3 of the Act shall submit to the competent customs office head the application under paragraph (1), accompanied by documents issued by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism proving the relevant facts, and obtain approval.

(3) Article 20 (8) shall apply mutatis mutandis to exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (2) 5 of the Act.

(4) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (2) 6 of the Act shall submit to the competent customs office head the application under paragraph (1), accompanied by documents issued by the inspection institution proving collection of the liquor, and obtain approval.

(5) A person seeking exemption from liquor tax on liquor under Article 20 (2) 7 of the Act shall submit to the competent customs office head the application under paragraph (1), accompanied by documents issued by the competent tax office head pursuant to Article 20 (1) proving that tax exemption was approved for the liquor as liquor for export, and obtain approval.

(6) Where the competent customs office head grants approval of exemption from liquor tax pursuant to paragraph (5), the customs office head shall notify the tax office head having jurisdiction over the liquor manufacturing premises of that approval.

(7) A person who has been exempted from liquor tax pursuant to paragraph (5) shall return the liquor to the manufacturing premises within seven days from the date on which the import declaration is accepted.

Article 24 (Collection of Liquor Tax for Failure to Meet Submission Deadlines)
Liquor tax shall be collected where documents required to be submitted pursuant to Article 15 (1) or (2) or Article 20 (2) or (3) are not submitted by the designated deadline.

Article 25 (Types of Security for Tax Payment)
The types of security under Article 20 (4) of the Act shall be limited to those prescribed in subparagraphs 1 through 3 of Article 18 of the National Tax Collection Act.

CHAPTER VI SECURITY FOR TAX PAYMENT

Article 26 (Designation and Change of Security Amounts and Periods)
(1) Where the competent tax office head deems it necessary to secure collection of liquor tax pursuant to Article 21 of the Act, the tax office head may order a liquor manufacturer to provide security for liquor tax or retain liquor, specifying the amount (meaning the value of the security or the price of the liquor to be retained as security for tax payment) and the period (meaning the date on which the order takes effect and the period during which the liquor is to be retained).

(2) The competent tax office head may change the amount or period under paragraph (1) where deemed necessary on grounds analogous to those under Article 21 (2) of the National Tax Collection Act.

Article 27 (Retention of Liquor and Methods Thereof)
(1) A person ordered to retain liquor pursuant to Article 26 shall determine the liquor to be retained and the method of retention and submit an application to the competent tax office head.

(2) The competent tax office head may seal liquor retained as security for tax payment.

Article 28 (Price of Liquor Used as Security for Tax Payment)
The price of liquor used to determine the value equivalent to the amount of liquor tax for the purpose of providing security for tax payment under Article 21 of the Act shall be the ordinary price.

Article 29 (Measures for Retention of Liquor Used as Security for Tax Payment)
Where the competent tax office head has ordered the provision of security or the retention of liquor pursuant to Article 26 and the liquor manufacturer has neither provided security nor applied for retention of liquor under Article 27 by the beginning of the period under Article 26 (1), the tax office head may seal liquor at the liquor manufacturing premises and prohibit its disposition or removal.

Article 30 (Questions and Inspections by Tax Officials)
Where a public official asks questions or conducts an inspection pursuant to Article 25 (1) of the Act, the official shall present identification indicating the official's authority to the persons concerned.

Article 31 (Processing of Unique Identifying Information)
The Commissioner of the National Tax Service, a tax office head, or a customs office head may process materials containing resident registration numbers or passport numbers under Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act where unavoidable to perform duties concerning exemption from liquor tax on the importation of liquor falling under any subparagraph of Article 20 (2) of the Act.

CHAPTER VII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 32 (Criteria for Imposition of Administrative Fines)
(1) The criteria for imposition of administrative fines under Article 27 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 5.

(2) The competent tax office head may reduce or increase the amount of an administrative fine prescribed in attached Table 5 by up to one-half, taking into consideration the severity and frequency of violations, the motive for the violation, its consequences, etc.: Provided, That, where the amount of an administrative fine is increased, it shall not exceed the maximum amount of the administrative fine under Article 27 (1) of the Act.

